      {
        "id": 38354,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-11-15T01:02:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Foote\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/48/1877_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Foote:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Brain Burn is certainly an extremely situational card. I have only ever played it seriously on a few occassions, and they generally invloved either the Chairman, Dawn, or an Advanced OG Blade who has a scary good first 2 rounds.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>It's also often useful against Omnitron, short-circuits several of Apostate's more annoying cards (though it's risky since he might Profane Summon into the redecked Relics, rather than getting a Relic Spirit killed and move them from the trash), and was extremely good in a recent La Capitan fight (\"All Together Now!&nbsp; Wait, why can't I remember where I parked my ship?\").&nbsp; Against Voss it can cancel Forced Deployment if the timing is just right, and The Matriarch is mentioned below.&nbsp; The Dreamer and Ambuscade shuffle their trash into their deck a lot anyway, so it does basically nothing but help them; it's a terrible idea against Gloomweaver (unless his trash has no Relics and you just want to stop a Vast Following of Cultists and Zombies), and does pretty much nothing against the rest of the villains (besides kill Visionary, which might occasionally be desireable if you want her incap ability sooner and she's clearly got a short lifespan anyhow - I could especially see this happening if Brain Burn is the only card in her hand, and an incapped hero earlier in the turn order has no more useful options than letting Visi play a card, which kills her and thus gives her things to do on her turn besides Enlighten someone).</p>\n<p>So, off the top of my head, Brain Burn is decent-to-excellent against 8 of the 18 villains, irrelevant for 7 of them, and largely counterproductive for 3.&nbsp; A better than 1/3 chance of the card being useful, combined with the ability to discard it for extra cards (unless you play Dark Visionary, which IMO you shouldn't), is not bad…but I do think it ought to have been a singleton, as I've drawn two copies in my starting hand in a distinctly excessive percentage of my games with her.&nbsp; I doubt that there are ten games, out of the nearly 6000 documented in the statistics thread, where you wanted to play Brain Burn <em>twice</em>…there's precedent for &gt;G changing card quantity in decks (Absolute Zero), so if I were hired on by them, one of the first things I'd try to do is replace one Brain Burn with an extra copy of Foresight or something (ideally I'd also give Foresight the text \"When this card comes into play, you may draw a card and you may play a card\"…given how it compares to her innate power, I very seldom want to use it, and making it not cost your play anymore would make it far more acceptible as an \"I'll use this if I really need it\" option among her several Powers).</p>\n<p>[/quote]</p>\n<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"phantaskippy\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/48/1897_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> phantaskippy:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unless we get a ruling that trash order cannot be alterred by players this will be an option, with the amount of cards that shuffle the trash before revealling I don't expect that ruling to ever be made.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>We do have at least one case where trash order needs to be preserved: Lost in the Past.&nbsp; If there's anything that shuffles the Environment trash, that messes with Silver Gulch in a big way.&nbsp; But yes, villains shuffle their trash often enough that an effect requiring villain decks to preserve trash order is unlikely to ever exist, unless some new villain deck itself contains that as a gimmick.</p>",
        "post_number": 30,
        "post_type": 1,
